{"builder":"Add a --tsv flag to the CLI so it reads tab-separated input. The pull request must meet both of these acceptance lines:\n1. \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\n2. \"README.md documents --tsv\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js and test/csv.test.js. `parse(text, sep = ',')` in src/parse.js already takes a separator argument, so you should not need to change the parser.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `--tsv` is in `process.argv.slice(2)`. If it is, call `parse(input, '\\t')`. If not, keep `parse(input)`, so running without flags behaves exactly as it does now. Stdin reading and the output format (`JSON.stringify(...)` followed by a newline) stay the same.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a CLI test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with the input `'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n'` and asserts the output is `'[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'`. That is the same output the existing CSV CLI test expects. Keep the existing tests.\n4. In README.md, document the flag: add a usage example such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and a sentence saying that --tsv reads tab-separated input instead of CSV. The literal text `--tsv` must appear in README.md.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes. Then check by hand:\n   d=$(mktemp -d); printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,,Paris\\n' > \"$d/t.csv\"; printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t\\tParis\\n' > \"$d/t.tsv\"; node bin/csv2json.js < \"$d/t.csv\" > \"$d/csv.out\"; node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < \"$d/t.tsv\" > \"$d/tsv.out\"; cmp \"$d/csv.out\" \"$d/tsv.out\" && echo SAME\n   The command must print SAME.\n6. Commit the changes and open one pull request that references https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run each command from the repository root. There is one check per acceptance line, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  d=$(mktemp -d); printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,,Paris\\n' > \"$d/t.csv\"; printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t\\tParis\\n' > \"$d/t.tsv\"; node bin/csv2json.js < \"$d/t.csv\" > \"$d/csv.out\"; node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < \"$d/t.tsv\" > \"$d/tsv.out\"; echo \"exit=$?\"; cat \"$d/csv.out\" \"$d/tsv.out\"; cmp \"$d/csv.out\" \"$d/tsv.out\" && echo SAME || echo DIFF\nPASS: the --tsv run prints exit=0, the CSV output is a JSON array with the rows of the table (here [{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":\"36\",\"city\":\"London\"},{\"name\":\"Bob\",\"age\":\"\",\"city\":\"Paris\"}]), and the command prints SAME, meaning the --tsv output is byte-identical to the CSV output.\nFAIL: the --tsv run exits non-zero, the command prints DIFF (for example, keys that still contain tab characters), or either output is empty or not a JSON array.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun:\n  grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md; echo \"grep_exit=$?\"\nThen read the matching lines.\nPASS: grep_exit=0, and at least one matching line describes --tsv as the option or usage for reading tab-separated input, for example `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` or a sentence explaining the flag.\nFAIL: grep_exit=1, or --tsv appears only in a way that does not document the flag (for example, a stray mention that does not say what it does or how to use it).\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- Run `npm test`. All tests, including the existing CSV tests, should pass, and a test for --tsv is ideally present.\n- Running `node bin/csv2json.js` without flags should behave exactly as before."}